 # Regularization losses
-
-Also called: Regularization terms, Regularization objectives
-
 ## Overview
-Regularization losses are auxiliary objective functions added to the primary photometric reconstruction loss to constrain the optimization of radiance field parameters (density, geometry, color, or Gaussian attributes). They solve the ill-posed nature of inverse volume rendering by preventing overfitting, suppressing visual artifacts (such as "floaters" or "holes"), and enforcing physical or structural priors [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods like [Photometric Reconstruction Loss](7799-photometric-reconstruction-loss.md) which focus on pixel-level fidelity, regularization losses target the internal consistency of the scene representation or the statistical properties of the output distribution [S1, S2, unofficial].
-
+Regularization losses are auxiliary terms added to the optimization objective of differentiable volume rendering to constrain the solution space, prevent overfitting, and stabilize training. They solve the ill-posed nature of inverse volume rendering by enforcing priors such as spatial smoothness, geometric compactness, or physical plausibility [S1, unofficial]. This family is distinguished from [Photometric Reconstruction Loss](7799-photometric-reconstruction-loss.md) by targeting the scene representation's structure rather than pixel-wise color fidelity, and from [Optimization Loops and Training Procedures](5622-optimization-loops-and-training-procedures.md) by focusing on the mathematical form of the penalty rather than the scheduling of updates.
 ## Shared principles
 All methods in this family rely on the differentiability of the forward rendering operator and the propagation of gradients through the integration steps [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. The core mechanism is the evaluation of the volume rendering equation and the computation of gradients with respect to scene parameters (density, geometry, transfer functions, camera poses) [44-radiance-field-optimization.md].
