+### Masked Opacity Regularization
+#### definition
+- [S2, unofficial] A regularization loss applied to prevent large Gaussians from interfering with visibility tests and causing gradient explosion, by pushing opacity outside a binary mask to zero while leaving opacity inside the mask unconstrained.
+#### pitfall
+- [S2, unofficial] Conventional mask regularization (pushing opacity inside mask to one) hurts BRDF parameter training and visibility tests; this method avoids that by only constraining opacity outside the mask.
 
 ## Background
-Regularization in volume rendering originated from the need to stabilize the optimization of sparse and implicit representations, where the inverse problem is highly ill-posed. Early works in neural radiance fields (NeRF) introduced noise injection and total variation terms to mitigate the "floaters" artifact [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. The field has since expanded to include perceptual losses for visual quality [S1, unofficial], geometric constraints for mesh extraction [S1, unofficial], and probabilistic priors for uncertainty estimation [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Modern approaches often combine multiple regularization terms into a total loss function to balance photometric accuracy with structural integrity [S1, S2, S3][S4, unofficial].
+Regularization in volume rendering emerged as a response to the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering, where multiple scene configurations can produce identical 2D projections. Early methods focused on spatial smoothness to suppress artifacts in sparse representations [S1, unofficial]. The introduction of Distortion Loss [S1, unofficial][S2, S4, unofficial] and Proposal Supervision [S1, unofficial] marked a shift towards geometric compactness and sampling efficiency. More recently, methods like Output Noise Regularization [S1, unofficial] and Uncertainty Priors [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial] have addressed overfitting in neural representations, while specialized losses like Forplane [S1, unofficial] and Joint Optimization [S1, unofficial] target specific representation structures like orthogonal planes and surfels.
 
